{{smaller block|Living in some ''under-stair'' office, when he [vainglorious man] would visit the country, he borrows some gallant’s cast suit of his servant, and therein, player-like, acts that part among his besotted neighbours.
{{right|''Rev. T. Adams'', Works, I. 500.}}}}

¶
'''understand''' (un-dėr-stand′), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''understood'', ppr. ''understanding''. [< ME. ''understanden'', ''understonden'', ''onderstanden'', ''onderstonden'' (pret. ''understod'', pp. ''understanden'', ''understonden'', also ''understande'', ''understonde'', and with weak ending ''understanded''), < AS. ''understandan'', ''understondan'' (= OFries. ''understonda'' = OHG. ''understantan'' = Icel. ''undirstanda'', understand (cf. D. ''onderstaen'', stand under, undertake), = MHG. ''unterstan'', G. ''unterstehen'' = Dan. ''understaa'', undertake, venture, intervene, hinder, resist), < ''under'', under, + ''standan'', stand: see ''under-'' and ''stand''.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To receive from a word or collocation of words or from a sign the idea it is intended to convey: with the thing said, the person speaking, or the language as the direct object of the verb.
{{smaller block|<poem>Speketh so pleyn at this tyme, I yow preye,
That we may ''understonde'' what ye seye.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Clerk’s Tale, Prol., l. 20.}}
<poem>Speak pardon, as ’tis current in our land,
The chopping French we do not ''understand''.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Rich. II., v. 3.}}
You shew your English Breeding now; an English Rival is so dull and brutish as not to ''understand'' Raillery.
{{right|''Wycherley'', Gentleman Dancing-Master, v. 1.}}}}
'''2.''' To interpret the signification of; seize the idea of; comprehend as resulting from a thought, principle, or rule; explain.
{{smaller block|I have heard say of thee, that thou canst ''understand'' a dream to interpret it. {{right|Gen. xli. 15.}}}}

readings.c1s3:
¶
{{smaller block|Can any ''understand'' the spreading of the clouds or the noise of his tabernacle?
{{right|Job xxxvi. 29.}}}}

'''3.''' To receive information about; learn by paying heed to what is said and done; consider.

{{smaller block|Zee schulle ''undirstonde'' that, aftre the opynyoun of olde wise Philosophres and Astronomeres, oure Contree ne Irelond ne Wales ne Scotlond ne Norweye ne the other Yles costynge to hem ne ben not in the superficyalte cownted aboven the Erthe.
{{right|''Mandeville'', Travels, p. 186.}}}}

{{smaller block|<poem>I haue ''vnderstande'', And by neighbours knowe,
That largely ye haue children good and fin.</poem>
{{right|''Rom. of Partenay'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 72.}}}}

{{smaller block|The heart also of the rash shall ''understand'' knowledge.
{{right|Isa. xxxii. 4.}}}}

{{smaller block|I hope to hear from you soon, for I long to ''understand'' how you fare.
{{right|''Winthrop'', Hist. New England, I. 416.}}}}

{{smaller block|''Understand'' the matter, and consider the vision.
{{right|Dan. ix. 23.}}}}

'''4.''' To know in substance, as a fact or saying; be acquainted with; recognize.

{{smaller block|<poem>This knowen, that his hestes ''understondeth'',
How that the second heste of God is that.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Pardoner’s Tale.}}}}

{{smaller block|What knoweth thou that we know not? what ''understandeth'' thou which is not in us?
{{right|Job xv. 9.}}}}

{{smaller block|Whom shall he teach knowledge? and whom shall he make to ''understand'' doctrine?
{{right|Isa. xxviii. 9.}}}}

'''5.''' To take as meant or implied; imply; infer; assume; take for granted: chiefly in the past participle.

readings.c2s1:
{{smaller block|<poem>War,
Open or ''understood'', must be resolved.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., i. 662.}}}}
'''6.''' To recognize as implied or meant, although not expressed; supply mentally, as a word necessary to bring out the sense of an author: as, in the phrase ‘All are mortal,’ we must ''understand'' the word ''men, living beings'', or the like.
{{smaller block|If you say to your grandmother “Ma’am, it’s a fine day,” or what not, she would find in the words no other meaning than their outward and visible one; but say so to the girl you love, and she ''understands'' a thousand mystic meanings in them.
{{right|''Thackeray'', Fitz-Boodle’s Confessions, Dorothea.}}}}
'''7.''' To stand under. [A punning use.]
{{smaller block|My legs do better ''understand'' me, sir, than I understand what you mean.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. N., iii. 1. 89.}}}}
'''To give to understand, to let understand, to make understand,''' to tell; inform; let know.
{{smaller block|To ''make'' you ''understand'' this in a manifested effect.
{{right|''Shak.'', M. for M., iv. 2. 169.}}}}
'''To have to understand†''', to learn; be informed. ''Shak.'', 3 Hen. VI., iv. 4. 10.—'''To understand trap.''' See ''trap''<sup>1</sup>.

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To have the use of the intellectual faculties; be an intelligent and conscious being; have understanding; be wise.
{{smaller block|What a fry of fools is here? I see ’tis treason to ''understand'' in this house.
{{right|''Shirley and Fletcher'', Coronation, i. 1.}}
[The] man that is in honour, and ''understandeth'' not, is like the beasts that perish.
{{right|Ps. xlix. 20.}}}}
'''2.''' To be informed by another; learn.
{{smaller block|I came to Jerusalem, and ''understood'' of the evil that Eliashib did.
{{right|Neh. xiii. 7.}}}}
'''3†.''' To give attention; listen.
{{smaller block|''Vndirstonde'' to me, kynge fflualis, and here the be-tokenynge of thyn a-vision.
{{right|''Merlin'' (E. E. T. S.), iii. 633.}}}}

readings.c2s2:
¶
'''understandable''' (un-dėr-stan′da-bl), ''a.'' [< ''understand'' + ''-able''.] That can be understood; capable of being understood; comprehensible; intelligible.
{{smaller block|To be ''understandable'' is a condition requisite to a judge.
{{right|''Chillingworth'', A Safe Way to Salvation.}}}}

'''understander''' (un-dėr-stan′dėr), ''n.'' [< ''understand'' + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] One who understands or knows.
{{smaller block|He [the critic of Homer] should rather (with his much better ''understander'' Spondanus) submit where he oversees him faulty.
{{right|''Chapman'', Iliad, i., Com.}}}}

'''understanding''' (un-dėr-stan′ding), ''n.'' [< ME. ''understanding'', ''understondynge'', ''onderstondinge'', etc.; verbal n. of ''understand'', ''v.''] '''1.''' The act of one who understands or comprehends; comprehension; apprehension and appreciation; discernment.
{{smaller block|The children of Issachar, which were men that had ''understanding'' of the times.
{{right|1 Chron. xii. 32.}}
A chaplain came up to him [Captain Whitock], to whom he delivered an account of his ''understanding'', and, I hope, of his belief, and soon after died; and my lord hath buried him with his own ancestors.
{{right|''Donne'', Letters, xx.}}}}
'''2.''' The knowing power, in general; intelligence; wit. {{smaller|The old psychologists divided the faculties of the mind into ''understanding'', or cognitive power, and ''will''.}}
{{smaller block|''Vnderstondynge'', yn wytte. Intelligencia, intellectus.
{{right|''Prompt. Parv.'', p. 511.}}
The spirit of wisdom and ''understanding'', the spirit of counsel and might, the spirit of knowledge and the fear of the Lord.
{{right|Isa. xi. 2.}}}}

readings.c2s3:
¶
{{smaller block|The power of perception is that which we call the ''understanding.'' Perception, which we make the act of the ''understanding,'' is of three sorts: 1. The perception of ideas in our mind. 2. The perception of the signification of signs. 3. The perception of the connection or repugnancy, agreement or disagreement, that there is between any of our ideas. All these are attributed to the ''understanding,'' or perceptive power, though it be the two latter only that use allows us to say we understand.
{{right|''Locke,'' Human Understanding, II. xxi. § 5.}}}}

{{smaller block|A spirit is one simple undivided active being: as it perceives ideas, it is called the ''understanding,'' as it produces or otherwise operates about them, it is called the will.
{{right|''Berkeley,'' Human Knowledge, i. § 27.}}}}

'''3.''' The representative faculty; the power of abstract thought; the logical power. {{smaller|Kantian writers restrict understanding to the operation of abstractive thought concerning objects of possible experience.}}

{{smaller block|And thus we discover a power we have of heightening the colour of our ideas, of changing or directing their course by the application of our notice: and the exercise of this power I take to be what is commonly meant by an act of the ''understanding.''
{{right|''A. Tucker,'' Light of Nature, xii. § 1.}}}}

{{smaller block|As all acts of the ''understanding'' can be reduced to judgments, the ''understanding'' may be defined as the faculty of judging. For we saw before that the ''understanding'' is the faculty of thinking, and thinking is knowledge by means of concepts.
{{right|''Kant,'' Critique of Pure Reason (tr. by Müller), II. 61.}}}}

'''4.''' Intelligence between two or more persons; agreement of minds; harmony; union of sentiment; also, something mutually understood or agreed upon: as, there was an ''understanding'' between them.

{{smaller block|I love to promote among my Clients a good ''Understanding.''
{{right|''Steele,'' Tender Husband, v. 1.}}}}

readings.c3s1:
¶
{{smaller block|Their once flaming regard is sobered by time in either breast, and, losing in violence what it gains in extent, it becomes a thorough good ''understanding''.
{{right|''Emerson'', Essays, 1st ser., p. 169.}}}}

{{smaller block|'''Men of Understanding''', a sect which flourished in the Low Countries about 1411, professing doctrines similar to those of the Brethren of the Holy Spirit. It maintained that the then present reign of the Holy Spirit afforded a higher illumination and authority than that of the Scripture; that the only resurrection of the body ever to take place had already taken place in Christ; and that the spirit is not defiled by bodily sin.—'''Predicables of the pure understanding.''' See ''predicable.''}}

'''understanding''' (un-dėr-stan′ding), ''p. a.'' Knowing; skilful; intelligent; possessed of or exhibiting good sense.
{{smaller block|<poem>Was this taken
By any ''understanding'' pate but thine?</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', W. T., i. 2. 223.}}
Monsieur d’Azout was very Curious and ''Understanding'' in Architecture, for which purpose he was 17 years in Italy by times.
{{right|''Lister'', Journey to Paris, p. 99.}}}}

'''understandingly''' (un-dėr-stan′ding-li), ''adv.'' In an understanding manner; intelligently; with full knowledge or comprehension.
{{smaller block|Your grace shall find him, in your further conference, grave, wise, courtly, and scholar-like, ''understandingly'' read in the necessities of the life of man.
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Woman-Hater, ii. 1.}}}}

'''understandingness†''', ''n.'' [ME. ''understondingnesse''; < ''understanding'' + ''-ness.''] The faculty of understanding.

'''understate''' (un-dėr-stāt′), ''v.'' '''I.''' ''trans.'' To state or represent less strongly than the truth will admit; state too low: as, to ''understate'' an evil.
{{smaller block|Rather ''understated'' for so high an honour.
{{right|''Fuller'', Worthies, Bedfordshire.}}}}
